<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inishowen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inishowen is the largest peninsula in Ireland, in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">County Donegal</a> between Lough Swilly and Lough Foyle, ending at Malin Head, the most northerly point of the island. Its name means 'Eógan's island', after the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenel-neogain">Cenél nEógain</a>, whose royal fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grianan-of-aileach">Grianan of Aileach</a> stands near its southern edge.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inishowen.jpg" alt="Inishowen" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Angr · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inishowen.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Inishowen covers 884.33 square kilometres at the northern end of the island of Ireland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> bounds it on the north, Lough Foyle on the east and Lough Swilly on the west, and it joins the rest of the island at the south. After the last ice age it was an island. Most of the population lives round the coast, while the interior is low mountain country, mostly bogland, rising to Slieve Snaght at 619 metres.</p><p>Most of the peninsula lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">County Donegal</a>, but its south-eastern part lies in County Londonderry, having been transferred from Donegal at the request of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> companies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a> in the early 17th century. Lough Swilly is a fjord-like lough that was of strategic importance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> as a deep-water harbour, and it is the departure point of the Flight of the Earls. Lough Foyle is shallower and leads to the river Foyle and the city of Derry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was named Inis Eoghain after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eogan-mac-neill">Eógan mac Néill</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niall-of-the-nine-hostages">Niall of the Nine Hostages</a>, and the name was also used for Tyrone. It was the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-lochlainn-dynasty">Meic Lochlainn</a>, descended from the tribe of Eoghan, a clan that grew strong enough that a king from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> came north to Aileach and ordered the fort destroyed, each of his soldiers carrying away a stone so that it could not be rebuilt. Ptolemy's Geography of the second century describes a point called Wenniknion, which probably referred to Inishowen.</p><p>After the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-lochlainn-dynasty">Meic Lochlainn</a> the Norman Earldom of Ulster expanded into Inishowen and founded Greencastle. In 1196 the Anglo-Norman knight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-de-courcy">John de Courcy</a> defeated the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenel-conaill">Cenél Conaill</a>, and two years later he returned to devastate Inishowen, but he was defeated in later campaigns by Clan O'Neill under its chief Áed Méith. After the Bruce invasion of the early 14th century the Ó Dochartaigh clan gradually conquered the peninsula, having lost their own homeland in the Laggan. In 1608 Sir Cahir O'Doherty, lord of Inishowen, began O'Doherty's Rebellion by burning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a>, and after its defeat most of the clan's territory was awarded to Arthur Chichester.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Inishowen includes Malin Head, the most northerly point of Ireland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2016 counted 40,544 people, of whom 6,785 lived in the urban area of Buncrana, the largest town. Its monuments include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grianan-of-aileach">Grianán of Aileach</a>, a ringfort restored in the nineteenth century, and the castle ruins at Greencastle, Buncrana, Elagh and Inch and Carrickabraghey on the Isle of Doagh. The peninsula is a starting or ending point of the Wild Atlantic Way.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inishowen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It took its name from the hilltop stone fort of Grianan of Aileach on the <b>Inishowen</b> peninsula, which served as a royal centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ailech">Kingdom of Ailech</a></li></ul></section>
